<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welsh people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Welsh are the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, a Celtic nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, whose name for themselves, Cymry, comes from a Brittonic word meaning &quot;fellow countrymen&quot;. They descend from the Britons who kept their language after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlements, and Welsh is today the most widely spoken Celtic language. Early modern antiquarians wrongly linked the name Cymry with the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, a theory long rejected.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welsh-people.jpg" alt="Welsh people" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Vector graphics by Tobias Jakobs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wales.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
